      "content": "El Niño threatens food security despite wheat production gains | Capital Newspaper Sign in Join Home Capital News Editorial Interview Society Opinion Business & Economy More Vacancy & Bid Arts and Culture Sports Entrepreneurs ispot Discourse with Dr. Desta Doing Business Advertisement አማርኛ Sign in Welcome! Log into your account your username your password Forgot your password? Create an account About Sign up Welcome! Register for an account your email your username A password will be e-mailed to you. About Password recovery Recover your password your email Search Sign in Welcome! Log into your account your username your password Forgot your password? Get help Create an account About Create an account Welcome! Register for an account your email your username A password will be e-mailed to you. About Password recovery Recover your password your email A password will be e-mailed to you. Monday, October 26, 2026 Login Facebook Instagram Linkedin Telegram TikTok Twitter Youtube Home Capital News Editorial Interview Society Opinion Business & Economy More Vacancy & Bid Arts and Culture Sports Entrepreneurs ispot Discourse with Dr. Desta Doing Business Advertisement አማርኛ Search Facebook Instagram Linkedin Telegram TikTok Twitter Youtube Home Capital News Editorial Interview Society Opinion Business & Economy More Vacancy & Bid Arts and Culture Sports Entrepreneurs ispot Discourse with Dr. Desta Doing Business Advertisement አማርኛ Search Capital News 3 min. Read El Niño threatens food security despite wheat production gains By Samuel Abate October 25, 2026 Facebook Twitter Pinterest WhatsApp Ethiopia’s efforts to expand wheat production and strengthen food security are facing a new challenge as El Niño-related weather risks threaten harvests and livelihoods, with humanitarian agencies warning that vulnerable communities need early support. More than 163 woredas have been identified as vulnerable to El Niño, with 114 woredas, home to an estimated 9.3 million people, designated for anticipatory action by the Ethiopia Disaster Risk Management Commission. Of these, 84 woredas in Afar, Amhara, Somali and Tigray regions have been classified as Priority Levels 1 and 2, based on the severity of risks and the need for assistance. Speaking at the 2026 World Food Day celebration in Addis Ababa, held under the theme “Innovate Today, Nourish Tomorrow,” Zlatan Milišić, the World Food Programme’s (WFP) Representative and Country Director for Ethiopia, said the country had experienced dry conditions for several months, raising concerns about agricultural production. “Generally, a poor harvest is expected, certainly a worse harvest than over the last several years,” Milišić said. He said the WFP was working with the Ethiopian government and other partners to prepare for and respond to the potential effects of El Niño. These efforts include early-warning messages, financial assistance to help communities prepare, and support for areas facing humanitarian needs. According to Milišić, the impact is particularly visible in Amhara, Tigray and Afar, where the government and its partners are focusing their response. The WFP is supporting up to two million people in Ethiopia, depending on seasonal needs, while also investing in resilience measures designed to help communities withstand future shocks. The warnings come as Ethiopia seeks to build on gains in agricultural production and reduce its vulnerability to food shortages. Figures indicate that the area under wheat cultivation increased from 20,000 hectares in 2020 to 3.78 million hectares in 2025/26, while reported annual production reached 318.4 million quintals. These figures reflect the scale of the country’s agricultural ambitions. However, increased production alone may not protect farmers and pastoralists from weather-related disruptions. The effects of drought or irregular rainfall can extend beyond crop yields to household incomes, livestock, food availability and access to markets. Dr. Workicho Jateno, Assistant FAO Representative for Programmes in Ethiopia, said climate change, land degradation, water scarcity, biodiversity loss, economic volatility, conflict and displacement were placing interconnected pressures on the country’s agri-food system. He called for stronger use of scientific evidence, digital technology, traditional knowledge and locally developed solutions to improve agricultural resilience. “Better risk information must lead to timely action that protects lives and livelihoods,” Jateno said, stressing the importance of linking early-warning systems with financing and preparedness measures. He also highlighted opportunities to strengthen climate services, irrigation, improved seeds, sustainable soil management and agricultural planning. However, he said technology alone would not transform food systems without accessible services, capable institutions, skilled workers and adequate financing. The Ministry of Agriculture is also relying on social-protection and livelihood programmes to help vulnerable households withstand food-security shocks. Sentayehu Demissie, Head of the Ministry’s Food Security Coordination Office, told Capital that the Rural Productive Safety Net Programme was supporting eight million people vulnerable to poverty and various hazards across 10 regions and one city administration. She said the programme was designed not only to address the effects of El Niño but also to tackle broader food-security challenges by helping households improve productivity and move towards self-reliance. Activities include support for land development, vocational training, income-generating activities, savings and agricultural production. According to figures provided by the ministry, more than 862,000 beneficiaries received comprehensive training through the Family Livelihood Improvement Programme, with women accounting for 48 per cent of participants. More than 857,000 beneficiaries accumulated savings totalling 1.2 billion birr, while 862,252 received one-time cash assistance. The programme also supplied water-harvesting technologies to beneficiaries, including water pumps, and distributed urea fertiliser to support agricultural production. The ministry reported that these interventions enabled the production of 720,000 quintals of crops, vegetables and fruits. Sentayehu said the programme’s broader objective was to link immediate assistance with longer-term livelihood development, helping households move beyond subsistence activities towards more productive and sustainable sources of income. Dr. Mandefro Nigussie, Chief Executive Officer of the Agricultural Transformation Institute, said Ethiopia was investing in irrigation, mechanisation, improved agricultural markets and more resilient rural economies. He stressed that agricultural transformation requires better coordination across production, processing, distribution and consumption, alongside investment in climate-smart practices and digital agriculture. As the country responds to disasters such as El Niño, the effectiveness of these measures will depend on how quickly support reaches vulnerable communities and whether longer-term investments can protect farmers and pastoralists against future shocks. Hot this week ispot Production up, but the ‘cost’ variable weighs heavily July 3, 2022 0 Production is up in 2021 for the Italian agricultural... ispot Luminos Fund’s catch-up education programs in Ethiopia recognized July 3, 2022 0 The Luminos Fund has been named a top 10... ispot Well-planned cities essential for a resilient future in Africa concludes the World Urban Forum July 3, 2022 0 The World Urban Forum (WUF) concluded today with a... Local News Private sector deemed key to unlocking AfCFTA potential March 27, 2023 0 The private sector’s role is vital to fully unlock... Uncategorized #Capitalethiopia News In Brief August 8, 2021 August 13, 2021 0 Topics Advertisement AfricaNews AmharicNews Arts and Culture Banner Ads Business & Economy Capital News CapitalCheck Discourse with Dr, Desta Doing Business Editorial Entrepreneurs Feature Featured In Pictures Interview ispot Local News News Opinion Regional News Society Sports Vacancy & Bid Video More Opinion Ethiopia’s collective investment schemes 0 Pooling Birr, Sharing Growth: What the New Collective Investment... Opinion Partnership and interaction: The foundation of life, civilization, and sustainable development 0 Partnership and interaction are the fundamental principles upon which... Opinion Ethical leadership: Why postmodern skepticism threatens modern leadership 0 Among the many structural scaffoldings that sustain human society,... Capital News Mastercard deploys AI, Tokenization to strengthen digital payment security 0 Global payments technology company Mastercard is deploying artificial intelligence... Interview Global South E-Commerce expansion 0 As Ethiopia works to expand its digital economy and... Editorial Keep the fight against Polio alive 0 World Polio Day is a moment to celebrate one... Capital News ECTA refutes EUDR exclusion concerns, says over 99% of coffee farms deforestation-free 0 The Ethiopian Coffee and Tea Authority (ECTA) says more... Capital News Police recover nearly $1 million in crypto in billion-birr fraud case 0 The Ethiopian Federal Police says it has recovered US$965,508... Related Articles Ethiopia ranks 133 out of 143 in WJP Rule of Law Index Capital News October 25, 2026 Mastercard deploys AI, Tokenization to strengthen digital payment security Capital News October 25, 2026 ECTA refutes EUDR exclusion concerns, says over 99% of coffee farms deforestation-free Capital News October 25, 2026 Cooperatives prepare to enter investment sector under new proclamation Capital News October 25, 2026 Police recover nearly $1 million in crypto in billion-birr fraud case Capital News October 25, 2026 Popular Categories AfricaNews Local News ispot Capital News In Pictures Sports News Previous article Public enterprise reform or infringement on workers’ rights? EDITOR PICKS Keep the fight against Polio alive October 25, 2026 Ethiopia’s debt crisis: beyond the billions October 18, 2026 A cabinet must deliver, not merely govern October 11, 2026 POPULAR POSTS The Easter holiday amid fuel shortages: Sacrificing tomorrow for today’s meal April 11, 2026 Djibouti’s Presidential Election 2026: A vote of confidence in three decades of transformation April 11, 2026 Lawyers push for massive $58 billion compensation for Ethiopian Airlines crash victims’ families August 9, 2024 POPULAR CATEGORY AfricaNews 4580 Local News 2727 ispot 2199 Capital News 1382 In Pictures 1236 Sports 1155 News 1139 Arts and Culture 665 ABOUT US Capital is the longest-established private English newspaper in Ethiopia Contact us: info@capitalethiopia.com +251911226900 | +251911637862 | +251944732300 | +251116183253 FOLLOW US Facebook Instagram Linkedin Telegram TikTok Twitter © Newspaper WordPress Theme by TagDiv Home Capital News Editorial Interview Society Opinion Business & Economy More አማርኛ",
      "summary": "El Niño is posing a significant threat to global food security, despite recent gains in wheat production. The phenomenon, characterized by warmer ocean temperatures in the Pacific, is expected to disrupt weather patterns, leading to adverse effects on crop yields. While wheat production has seen improvements in some regions, the overall impact of El Niño could negate these gains, particularly in vulnerable areas. Experts warn that the increased likelihood of droughts and floods could severely affect agricultural output, exacerbating food shortages and inflation. Countries heavily reliant on wheat imports may face heightened risks, leading to potential social unrest. As nations prepare for the upcoming El Niño event, there is an urgent need for strategic planning and investment in agricultural resilience to mitigate the impending challenges to food security.",
