      "title": "The separatist projects in Somalia, Yemen, and Sudan and the power game in the Red Sea region - pism.pl",
      "link": "https://www.pism.pl/konferencje/the-separatist-projects-in-somalia-yemen-and-sudan-and-the-power-game-in-the-red-sea-region",
      "date": "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 10:43:07 GMT",
      "image": "",
      "content": "O nas O Instytucie Dyrekcja Rada PISM Struktura Praca w PISM Wystąpienia medialne Zamówienia Publiczne Kontakt Publikacje Analitycy Podcasty Wydarzenia Projekty Szkolenia Biblioteka Księgarnia Kontakt Kontrast A A PL EN Strona główna The separatist projects in Somalia, Yemen, and Sudan and the power game in the Red Sea region The separatist projects in Somalia, Yemen, and Sudan and the power game in the Red Sea region Początek spotkania: 6.11.2026, 12:30 Zakończenie spotkania: 6.11.2026, 14:00 The Brussels Office of the Polish Institute of International Affairs (PISM) is pleased to invite you to a roundtable discussion The separatist projects in Somalia, Yemen, and Sudan and the power game in the Red Sea region ___________________________ 6 November 2026 (Friday) 12:30 – 14:00 PISM office at the premises of Business & Science Poland, Rue Belliard 40, 5 th floor _____________________________ The discussion is organised in the context of the publication of the PISM report entitled “ New Flags in the Red Sea Region: Understanding the Acceleration of Separatist Projects in Somalia, Yemen and Sudan ” by Jędrzej Czerep, Julia Sochacka and Michał Wojnarowicz. As the political and security landscape in the wider Red Sea region evolves, three separatist projects have emerged as pivotal: In Sudan, the 'Tasis' coalition, supported by the Rapid Support Forces (RSF) militia and their political allies, formed an alternative government in Nyala, South Darfur, in July 2025. They began establishing their own quasi-state administration in the Darfur and Kordofan regions, effectively partitioning Sudan. In December 2025, Israel officially recognised Somaliland as an independent state, with its capital in Hargeisa. Somaliland’s authorities accelerated their outreach to the US government in an attempt to win recognition from Donald Trump’s administration. This hindered the progress of Somali-Somaliland negotiations on the future of the territory. Also in December 2025, the Yemeni separatist Southern Transitional Council (STC) took control of almost the entire territory of the former People's Democratic Republic of Yemen. In January 2026, the STC published a draft constitution for the projected State of South Arabia. However, efforts to restore independence to Yemen's southern provinces were swiftly suppressed by the central government, the Presidential Leadership Council, and Saudi Arabia. The security vacuum that resulted encouraged the Houthis to escalate. This regional wave of separatisms resulted from a combination of internal dynamics within individual states and the projection of influence by regional actors, particularly the UAE and Israel. These countries have sought to drastically reshape the regional security status quo and expand their influence. While there is no universal model of secession, the movements and their regional backers in each of the three cases seek to normalise unilateral changes to borders, adding a new strain on international law and multilateralism. For this reason, these processes remain highly relevant to the EU. Throughout this seminar, participants will discuss the internal and external factors that determine the rise and decline of separatist movements in the region, addressing the following questions: What is the current strength of separatist movements and secessionist governments in the region? What role do regional parties' foreign patrons and sponsors, such as the UAE, Israel or Saudi Arabia, play in accelerating or thwarting these projects? How do other actors, such as neighbouring states, international organisations and foreign stakeholders, approach this issue? What position should the EU and the US take regarding last year's developments around the Red Sea? Agenda: 12:00-12:30 Arrivals. Sandwich lunch. 12:30-12:35 Welcome and opening remarks Melchior Szczepanik, Head of Brussels Office, PISM 12:35-13:30 Panel Discussion Jędrzej Czerep, Head of the Middle East and Africa Programme, Polish Institute of International Affairs (PISM) Mihai Sebastian Chihaia, Policy Analyst, European Policy Centre Rossella Marangio, Senior Analyst, EU Institute for Strategic Studies (online) Julia Sochacka, Middle East Analyst, PISM Maike Tribbels, Head of Division, Horn of Africa, East Africa, European External Action Service Moderator: Melchior Szczepanik, Head of Brussels Office, PISM 13:30-14:00 Exchange of views with participants The debate will be held under the Chatham House Rule. To confirm attendance and for any inquiries, please reply to brusselsoffice@pism.pl by 2 November. Bądź na bieżąco z najnowszymi analizami PISM, zapisz się na newsletter Obserwuj nas na: O nas O Instytucie Dyrekcja Rada PISM Struktura PISM Praca w PISM Zamówienia Publiczne Kontakt Publikacje Biuletyn Raport Policy Paper Strategic File Książki Komentarz Polskie Dokumenty Dyplomatyczne Polski Przegląd Dyplomatyczny Yearbook of Polish Foreign Policy Rocznik Polskiej Polityki Zagranicznej Inne publikacje Analitycy Konferencje Podcasty Wystąpienia medialne Projekty Szkolenia Biblioteka Księgarnia Kontakt Prywatność i pliki cookie Deklaracja Dostępności © Polski Instytut Spraw Międzynarodowych Techniczne (obowiązkowe) Analityczne (nieobowiązkowe) Ta witryna korzysta z cookie, aby polepszyć doświadczenie przeglądania i zapewnić dodatkową funkcjonalność. Zaakceptuj wszystkie Odrzuć wszystkie Dostosuj",
      "summary": "The Polish Institute of International Affairs (PISM) is hosting a roundtable discussion on November 6, 2026, focusing on separatist movements in Somalia, Yemen, and Sudan, as well as the geopolitical dynamics in the Red Sea region. This event coincides with the release of a PISM report titled “New Flags in the Red Sea Region,” which analyzes the rise of separatist projects. Key developments include the formation of a quasi-state in Sudan by the 'Tasis' coalition, Somaliland's push for international recognition following Israel's acknowledgment, and the Southern Transitional Council's control over former Yemeni territories. These movements are influenced by internal state dynamics and external support from regional powers like the UAE and Israel, which aim to reshape the regional security landscape. The discussion will explore the implications of these separatist movements for international law and the responses of the EU and the US to these developments. Participants will engage in a debate under the Chatham House Rule.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/the-separatist-projects-in-somalia-yemen-and-sudan-and-the-p-cc0e2364de.html"
    },
